The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For $p > 1$, we analyze the $\ell_p$ norm of $x_n - c$. Since $x_n - c \in \ell_1$, its terms must eventually be less than 1 (as the $\ell_1$ norm is finite). For such terms, $|x_n - c|^p \leq |x_n - c|$ because $p > 1$ and $|x_n - c| \leq 1$. Thus, $\|x_n - c\|_p^p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$, implying $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$. Hence, convergence in $\ell_1$ guarantees convergence in $\ell_p$ for all $p > 1$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{True}

